{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>You shall not muzzle the ox.</b> Rashi's comment: to exclude the person — that is, to exempt him from the prohibition if he muzzled him, for you are not warned concerning him. And if you ask: derive it from ״ואכלת ענבים כנפשך שבעך״ (you may eat grapes to your fill, as you desire), since he is not liable to lashes for muzzling a person, as we say: what is your reasoning — if you muzzled, he is exempt, etc.? One may answer: both are necessary. For if the Merciful One wrote כנפשך but did not write שור, I would say: he is exempt from payment, since with an animal there is payment, as taught: one who muzzles a cow's mouth and threshes with it pays four kav to the cow, etc.; but one who muzzles a person is exempt from payment. But to exempt him from the prohibition that obligates lashes — we do not exempt him; therefore the Merciful One wrote שור to exclude the person, to exempt him from the prohibition. And if the Merciful One wrote שור but did not write כנפשך, we would not exempt him from payment but only from lashes. Therefore both are written: כנפשך and שור. <b>In its threshing.</b> Rashi's comment: why is threshing said, etc. — likewise everything similar to it, meaning that you have no permission to muzzle, neither ox nor person, in its threshing or anything similar to it; the milker and the cheese-maker are excluded, etc., meaning that the worker does not eat from them. Another interpretation: why is threshing said? One may say: just as threshing is distinguished — a thing whose work and growth from the land are not yet completed, so too everything similar to it, that grows from the land and whose work is not yet completed — you may not muzzle your ox during its labor. And we draw an analogy between the one who muzzles and the one who is muzzled: just as the one who is muzzled, namely the ox, is permitted to eat during the time of its threshing from a thing that grows from the land and whose work is not yet completed, so too the model of the one who muzzles, namely the person, is permitted to eat during the time of labor from a thing that grows from the land and whose work is not yet completed. For tithes, the milker and the cheese-maker are excluded, etc., that the worker does not eat from them even during the time of labor."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:54:17.687463278Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}